nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; We conjecture that all simplified de Bruijn graphs S(n,k)$S(n,k)$ are non-word-rerpesentable for n\\geq 4$n\\geq 4$ and k\\geq 3$k\\geq 3$.\nDe Bruijn graphs are a key object in combinatorics on words that found numerous applications, in particular, in genome assembly. We show that binary simplified de Bruijn graphs (i.e.\\ S(n,2)S(n,2)$S(n,2)$) are word-representable for any n\\geq 1 n≥1$n\\geq 1$, while S(2,k)$S(2,k)$ and S(3,k)$S(3,k)$ are non-word-representable for k\\geq 3$k\\geq 3$. We conjecture that all simplified de Bruijn graphs S(n,k)$S(n,k)$ are non-word-rerpesentable for n\\geq 4$n\\geq 4$ and k\\geq 3$k\\geq 3$.\n\nComments:  | 24 pages, 2 figures. arXiv admin note: text overlap with arXiv:2110.05405 by other authors\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2210.14762 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2210.14762v2 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2210.14762 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Anthony Petyuk [view email]\n[v1] Thu, 20 Oct 2022 07:50:43 UTC (145 KB)\n[v2] Wed, 12 Jul 2023 01:46:41 UTC (138 KB)\n\n\n## Access Paper:\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled On word-representability of simplified de Bruijn graphs, by Anthony V. Petyuk\n\n  * View PDF\n  * HTML (experimental)\n  * TeX Source\n\nview license--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMB_v18_12_Dimitrov (https://www.math.bas.bg/infres/MathBalk/MB-18/MB-18-129-140.pdf)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; In the present paper, we use the theory of modular group algebras and, for an arbitrary finite *p*-group \\(G\\), we show that \\(D(G)\\le L(G)\\), where \\(L(G)\\) is the Loewy length of \\(\\mathbb{F}_pG\\), i.e. the class of nilpotency of the Jacobson radical of \\(\\mathbb{F}_pG\\), with the obvious equality when \\(G\\) is abelian. ... Applying our methods, we prove our main result which is the strong version of the Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv theorem for arbitrary finite *p*-groups, answering in the affirmative a conjecture of Olson in the special case of *p*-groups.\nMathematica Balkanica  \nNew Series Vol. 18, 2004, Fasc. 1-2\n\nOn the Strong Davenport Constant  \nof Nonabelian Finite *p*-Groups\n\nVesselin Dimitrov\n\nFor a finite group \\(G\\), the strong Davenport constant \\(D(G)\\) is defined as the minimal integer \\(s\\) such that, for any sequence of (not necessarily pairwise different) elements \\(g_1,\\ldots,g_s \\in G\\), there exist indices \\(i_1 < \\cdots < i_k\\) with \\(g_{i_1}\\cdots g_{i_k}=1\\). In the present paper, we use the theory of modular group algebras and, for an arbitrary finite *p*-group \\(G\\), we show that \\(D(G)\\le L(G)\\), where \\(L(G)\\) is the Loewy length of \\(\\mathbb{F}_pG\\), i.e. the class of nilpotency of the Jacobson radical of \\(\\mathbb{F}_pG\\), with the obvious equality when \\(G\\) is abelian. We prove the equality \\(D(G)=L(G)\\) also when \\(|G|=p^3\\) and \\(p=3\\ (\\mathrm{mod}\\ 4)\\). Applying our methods, we prove our main result which is the strong version of the Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv theorem for arbitrary finite *p*-groups, answering in the affirmative a conjecture of Olson in the special case of *p*-groups.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv theorem constant of finite groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.21052)\nciteturn0academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Title: The Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv theorem constant of finite groups ... The Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv Theorem constant of the group $G$, denoted $\\mathsf E(G)$, is defined as the smallest positive integer $\\ell$ with the following property: for any given sequence $(g_1,\\ldots,g_{\\ell})$ over $G$, there exist $n$ distinct integers $i_1,\\ldots,i_n\\in \\{1,\\ldots,\\ell\\}$ such that the product of $g_{i_1},\\ldots,g_{i_n}$, in some order, is the identity element of $G$. ... In this paper, we confirm the conjecture for all non-cyclic groups $G$ whose order is not divisible by four, and characterize the groups achieving the equality $\\mathsf E(G)=\\frac{3|G|}{2}$ as those with a cyclic subgroup of index two.\nTitle: The Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv theorem constant of finite groups\nAuthors: Yang Zhao, Guoqing Wang\nDate: Sun Mar 22 04:21:48 2026\n\nLet $G$ be a multiplicatively written finite group of order $n$. The Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv Theorem constant of the group $G$, denoted $\\mathsf E(G)$, is defined as the smallest positive integer $\\ell$ with the following property: for any given sequence $(g_1,\\ldots,g_{\\ell})$ over $G$, there exist $n$ distinct integers $i_1,\\ldots,i_n\\in \\{1,\\ldots,\\ell\\}$ such that the product of $g_{i_1},\\ldots,g_{i_n}$, in some order, is the identity element of $G$. The Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv Theorem constant originates from the celebrated additive theorem proved by Erdős, Ginzburg and Ziv in 1961, which amounts to proving $\\mathsf E(G)\\leq 2|G|-1$ holds in case that $G$ is abelian. It is also well-known that $\\mathsf E(G)=2|G|-1$ holds for all finite cyclic groups. In 2010, Gao and Li [J. Pure Appl. Algebra] conjectured that $\\mathsf E(G)\\leq \\frac{3|G|}{2}$ for every finite non-cyclic group $G$. In this paper, we confirm the conjecture for all non-cyclic groups $G$ whose order is not divisible by four, and characterize the groups achieving the equality $\\mathsf E(G)=\\frac{3|G|}{2}$ as those with a cyclic subgroup of index two.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn word-representability of simplified de Bruijn graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.14762)\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.1 years ago; We conjecture that all simplified de Bruijn graphs $S(n,k)$ are non-word-rerpesentable for $n\\geq 4$ and $k\\geq 3$.\nTitle: On word-representability of simplified de Bruijn graphs\nAuthors: Anthony V. Petyuk\nDate: Thu Oct 20 07:50:43 2022\n\nA graph $G=(V,E)$ is word-representable if there exists a word $w$ over the alphabet $V$ such that letters $x$ and $y$ alternate in $w$ if and only if $xy\\in E$. Word-representable graphs generalize several important classes of graphs such as $3$-colorable graphs, circle graphs, and comparability graphs. There is a long line of research in the literature dedicated to word-representable graphs. In this paper, we study word-representability of simplified de Bruijn graphs. The simplified de Bruijn graph $S(n,k)$ is a simple graph obtained from the de Bruijn graph $B(n,k)$ by removing orientations and loops and replacing multiple edges between a pair of vertices by a single edge. De Bruijn graphs are a key object in combinatorics on words that found numerous applications, in particular, in genome assembly. We show that binary simplified de Bruijn graphs (i.e.\\ $S(n,2)$) are word-representable for any $n\\geq 1$, while $S(2,k)$ and $S(3,k)$ are non-word-representable for $k\\geq 3$. We conjecture that all simplified de Bruijn graphs $S(n,k)$ are non-word-rerpesentable for $n\\geq 4$ and $k\\geq 3$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombinatorial invariants for certain classes of non-abelian groups (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022314X25003233)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; In 1961, Erdős, Ginzburg, and Ziv [8] proved that $\\mathsf{E}(G)\\le 2|G|-1$ for any finite solvable group G, which is known as the Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv theorem. ... Recently, in 2021, Gao, Li, and Qu [11] modified the bound in Conjecture 1.2 and proved that $\\mathsf{E}(G)\\le \\frac{3}{2}(|G|-1)$ for a finite non-cyclic group G of odd order $|G|>9$.\nIn this connection, we have our first result, which is as follows:\n\nTheorem 1.1\n\nLet A be a finite abelian group. For the group $G=A{⋊}_{-1}{C}_{2}$, we have${\\mathsf{D}}_{o}(G)=\\mathsf{d}(G)+1=\\mathsf{d}(A)+2.$ Moreover, ${\\mathsf{D}}_{o}(G)=\\beta (G)$.\n\nOur next result is related to the Gao constant. In 1961, Erdős, Ginzburg, and Ziv [8] proved that $\\mathsf{E}(G)\\le 2|G|-1$ for any finite solvable group G, which is known as the Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv theorem. Later, in 1976, Olson [22] extended this result to any finite group G. Following their work, significant efforts were made to reduce the upper bound on $\\mathsf{E}(G)$ (see [27] and [9] for more details). In 1996, Gao established that $\\mathsf{E}(G)=\\mathsf{d}(G)+|G|$ for any finite abelian group G. Later on, Gao and Li [10], in 2010, refined the upper bound to $\\mathsf{E}(G)\\le \\frac{7}{4}|G|-1$ and proposed a conjecture:\n\nConjecture 1.2\n\n[10] For any finite non-cyclic group G, we have $\\mathsf{E}(G)\\le \\frac{3}{2}|G|$.\n\nIn 2015, Han [16] confirmed that Conjecture 1.2 holds for all finite non-cyclic nilpotent groups (see [15], [1], [17], and [24] for recent progress on the above conjectures). Recently, in 2021, Gao, Li, and Qu [11] modified the bound in Conjecture 1.2 and proved that $\\mathsf{E}(G)\\le \\frac{3}{2}(|G|-1)$ for a finite non-cyclic group G of odd order $|G|>9$.\n\nIn support of Conjecture 1.2, we prove the following result for $\\mathsf{E}(G)$:\n\nTheorem 1.3\n\nLet p be a prime and A be a finite abelian p-group. For the group $G=A{⋊}_{-1}{C}_{2}$, we have$\\mathsf{E}(G)\\le \\frac{3}{2}|G|.$\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Erd\\H{o}s-Ginzburg-Ziv theorem constant of finite groups (https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.21052)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; View a PDF of the paper titled The Erd\\H{o}s-Ginzburg-Ziv theorem constant of finite groups, by Yang Zhao and 1 other authors ... The Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv Theorem constant of the group G$G$, denoted E(G)$\\mathsf E(G)$, is defined as the smallest positive integer ℓ$\\ell$ with the following property: for any given sequence (g 1,…,